WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.850
SPEAKER: SQL has many built-in
functions for performing

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.720
calculations on data.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.240
But sometimes,
your systems might

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.030
need to handle data, such
as string or date values,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.890
in a unique way.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.140
User-defined functions
are an efficient way

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.990
to have these custom
calculations at your fingertips

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.520
when analyzing data.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.680
On today's episode of
BigQuery spotlight,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.350
we're talking about how to
create, store, and share

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.090
user-defined
functions in BigQuery.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.585
So stick around.

00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:28.435
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.100
So what is a User-Defined
Function, or UDF?

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.170
A UDF lets you create a reusable
function which you define,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.640
with either another SQL
expression or with JavaScript.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.520
These functions accept
input, perform actions,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.720
and then return a
value as a result.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.920
Once you've created a UDF, you
can reference it in queries

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.320
or when creating logical
views, just as you

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.500
would with built-in functions.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.940
Let's see an example use case.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.190
Imagine an application
that captures data

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.770
from various sources
and requires any text

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.510
fields to be cleansed
before they can be

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.330
used for analytical purposes.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:11.920
We can write one UDF--

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.920
we'll call it cleanse_string--
to tackle this data cleansing

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:15.640
problem.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.570
The SQL expression that defines
the UDF does three things.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.200
It trims white spaces,
converts text to lowercase,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.760
and removes symbols.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.173
After creating and
defining the UDF,

00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:28.840
in the next part of
the query statement,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.270
we can test three
different strings of text

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.890
through our new function.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.823
If we run the entire query.

00:01:34.823 --> 00:01:36.490
We will see the before
and after results

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:37.990
for each string of text.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.450
Once we are happy with the
function's performance,

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.880
we could add it to the query
statements that analyze data

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.280
from our application.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.280
This example includes what
we call a temporary UDF,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.770
meaning it is created
and used within one

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.780
single query statement.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.470
However, BigQuery also
supports persistent UDFs,

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.050
which are defined and stored
within a specific project

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:02.410
and data set, but can be
reused across multiple queries

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.180
and projects.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.940
Let's head into the console
and see how to create, use,

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.979
and share a persistent UDF.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:12.040
You can follow along in
your own BigQuery sandbox.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.020
If you don't have
one yet, set one up

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.030
following the video link below.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.000
It's good to note
that in the sandbox,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.810
your persistent UDFs
will remain indefinitely.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.670
So they aren't limited to
the same 60-day storage limit

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.560
that tables have
within the sandbox.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.060
Start in the Query Editor.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.280
Here you'll define
your UDF using SQL,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.950
following a specific syntax.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.200
CREATE OR REPLACE
FUNCTION indicates

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.140
that you would like to
create a persistent UDF.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.020
This is followed by your
project ID and data set name

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.690
where you want the UDF to live.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.790
Next is the name for
the function itself.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.425
In this case, cleanse_string.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.020
Following in parentheses
is a comma-separated list

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.390
of all input parameters
with their data types

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:57.640
that your UDF requires.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.350
For cleanse_string, you
have just one input,

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.780
which is a string of text.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.990
On the next line, the return
clause specifies the data type

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.430
that the function will return.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.160
Now it's time to provide a
SQL expression that defines

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.740
how to process the input.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.470
This expression uses
the SQL functions TRIM

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:20.120
to trim whitespace and LOWER to
convert the text to lowercase.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.160
Then it uses a
regular expression

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.020
to remove any symbols.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.460
Now you're ready to click Run
to create the persistent UDF.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.980
You will see a populate under
your data set in the left hand

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.060
nav.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.970
Selecting the UDF allows you
to view, edit, or delete it.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.420
But first, let's use it.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.640
We'll bring up the same
query that we showed earlier

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.140
in the video, but
this time we can

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.720
reference the persistent
UDF without needing

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.470
to provide its definition
within the same query statement.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.570
Going forward, you
can use this UDF

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.660
just like you would any
other SQL function, directly

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:56.210
within your query statements.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.620
You will need to reference
it with its full name,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.210
including the project and
data set in which it resides.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.670
In fact, any user with
the adequate permission

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:08.180
to the data set containing the
UDF, such as the BigQuery data

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.470
viewer role, can also
reference it in their queries.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.630
By sharing data sets,
you have the potential

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.350
to create an org-wide library of
UDFs, which in turn can ensure

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.290
business logic is applied
consistently and efficiently

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.330
across your organization.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.410
Now that you know how to create
and use UDFs in BigQuery,

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:30.090
it's time to start
writing some of your own.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.120
If you're looking for
inspiration or perhaps

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.970
to just not reinvent the wheel,
check out the Resources link

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:35.720
below.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:38.330
They include several
UDF examples, including

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.070
many written in JavaScript.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.920
Thanks for joining us today
for BigQuery spotlight.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.240
Watch out for the next episode.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.890
And remember, stay curious.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

